From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (agent session)" <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 18:22:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] renderer: D3D11 composition mode via a DirectComposition
 surface handle

Lets an embedder bind libghostty's output into its own compositor. The
renderer creates a DirectComposition surface handle, builds the swap chain
against it, and exposes the handle through
ghostty_surface_get_swap_chain_handle. A XAML SwapChainPanel consumes that
with ISwapChainPanelNative2::SetSwapChainHandle - which is exactly what
Windows Terminal already does in TermControl::_AttachDxgiSwapChainToXaml.

Verified end to end against harness/xaml-host, a system-XAML island host:
the panel displays libghostty's configured background (#282C34), resizes
cleanly from 540x430 to 1400x900 with no stale regions, and reports
CompositionScale 1.50.

The handle-based path is a different interface from the object-based one
------------------------------------------------------------------------
IDXGIFactory2::CreateSwapChainForComposition pairs with
ISwapChainPanelNative::SetSwapChain and takes a swap chain *object*. What WT
uses is IDXGIFactoryMedia::CreateSwapChainForCompositionSurfaceHandle paired
with ISwapChainPanelNative2::SetSwapChainHandle, which takes a HANDLE. Only
the object-based half was bound previously (it came with PR #11886, whose
demo used it), so IDXGIFactoryMedia and DCompositionCreateSurfaceHandle are
added here, harvested from deblasis/wintty (MIT) and attributed in-file.

The indirection is the point: the panel references the handle, not the swap
chain, so ResizeBuffers leaves the binding intact and a resize needs no
re-attach.

Resize has a different authority per mode
-----------------------------------------
hwnd mode reads the window's client rect. A composition surface has no window
to measure, so it is driven by whatever size the embedder set on the surface -
the plumbing the hwnd path had previously sidestepped. drawFrameStart now
picks the right authority for the mode.

Device loss needs a notification, not just a getter
---------------------------------------------------
Losing the device means a new swap chain and therefore a *new* composition
handle; an embedder still bound to the old one shows a frozen last frame,
which looks like a hang rather than a failure. A getter cannot express that,
because the embedder has no way to know it happened.

So this adds a swap_chain_changed apprt action, following the documented
procedure in action.zig - appended to the end of the Key enum for ABI
compatibility, mirrored into ghostty.h, and covered by the existing
checkGhosttyHEnum test. It travels from the renderer thread through the
surface mailbox, since action delivery belongs to the app thread.

Windows Terminal already models precisely this shape:
ControlCore::_renderEngineSwapChainChanged duplicates the renderer's new
handle and raises SwapChainChanged so TermControl re-binds.

Measured rather than assumed: forcing a rebuild produced handle 0x179C ->
0x21D0, so a new handle really is required. Device.recreate also re-walks the
full hardware->WARP ladder rather than pinning the previous rasterizer,
because a device can be lost precisely because the hardware went away.

GHOSTTY_D3D11_SIMULATE_DEVICE_LOSS=<n> forces the recovery path after n
frames. D3D11 has no equivalent of ID3D12Device::RemoveDevice and provoking a
real TDR means hanging the GPU, so this simulates the trigger. It exercises
our recovery, not the driver's - a real TDR also invalidates driver state, and
this hook would not catch a divergence there.

zig build -Dapp-runtime=none -> imports dcomp.dll alongside d3d11.dll
zig build test                -> 3065 passed, 55 skipped, 0 failed

